WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.478
In this part we solve two real problems.

2
00:00:03.478 --> 00:00:07.217
First: when will the tea be ready to drink?

3
00:00:07.217 --> 00:00:13.652
Second: when will a virus reach half of a network of a thousand computers?

4
00:00:13.652 --> 00:00:19.826
The plan is the same for both: write the model, separate the variables,

5
00:00:19.826 --> 00:00:25.478
find the coefficient from a measurement, and answer the question.

6
00:00:25.728 --> 00:00:33.588
Newton’s law: the cooling rate is proportional to the difference between the body and room temperatures.

7
00:00:33.588 --> 00:00:38.198
So T prime equals minus k times T minus the room temperature.

8
00:00:38.198 --> 00:00:41.070
The minus sign means a hot body cools.

9
00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.111
Separating and integrating,

10
00:00:43.111 --> 00:00:51.878
the difference decays exponentially: T equals room temperature plus the initial difference times e to the minus k t.

11
00:00:52.128 --> 00:00:59.596
The room is at twenty degrees, the tea is poured at ninety and is sixty after ten minutes.

12
00:00:59.596 --> 00:01:05.073
Substitute: T equals twenty plus seventy times e to the minus k t.

13
00:01:05.073 --> 00:01:10.384
At ten minutes, seventy times e to the minus ten k equals forty.

14
00:01:10.384 --> 00:01:18.350
So k is one tenth of the logarithm of seven quarters, about zero point zero five six per minute.

15
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.307
Now the question: when is it thirty degrees?

16
00:01:22.307 --> 00:01:30.059
We need seventy times e to the minus k t to equal ten, so e to the minus k t is one seventh.

17
00:01:30.059 --> 00:01:37.642
Hence t equals the logarithm of seven divided by k, about thirty four point eight minutes.

18
00:01:37.642 --> 00:01:42.950
Check: twenty plus seventy times one seventh is exactly thirty.

19
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.541
The second model is a virus in a network.

20
00:01:46.541 --> 00:01:57.134
The infection rate is proportional to contacts between infected and healthy machines: I prime equals beta times I times N minus I.

21
00:01:57.134 --> 00:01:59.578
This is the logistic equation.

22
00:01:59.578 --> 00:02:04.386
Its solution is N over one plus A times e to the minus r t.

23
00:02:04.386 --> 00:02:09.601
With a thousand computers, ten at first and fifty after an hour,

24
00:02:09.601 --> 00:02:13.838
half are infected after about two point eight hours.

25
00:02:14.088 --> 00:02:17.912
The graph shows the number of infected computers.

26
00:02:17.912 --> 00:02:25.093
At first the growth is almost exponential, then the curve bends and approaches one thousand.

27
00:02:25.093 --> 00:02:31.337
The inflection point is exactly at five hundred, when the virus spreads fastest.

28
00:02:31.337 --> 00:02:36.566
That is why protection must be applied before the inflection point.

29
00:02:36.816 --> 00:02:38.493
Check question.

30
00:02:38.493 --> 00:02:44.309
Choose when growth is fastest in the logistic model.

31
00:02:44.309 --> 00:02:48.782
Hint: the right side is a parabola in P.
